ng to know her  and loved her as a character personally  I do I know not everyone does but I like  River a lot so at this point going back  and rewatching that episode that moment  hits me much harder than it did before  and if you consider that unfair I don't  care it still hits me really hard so I'm  gonna talk about it I think a big part  of it for me  is just this kind of realization that  she has that she is going to die but  then also this this combination of  sadness and acceptance because when the  doctor says you know time can be  rewritten and she just lashes out at him  not those not those times not one word  don't you dare it is this moment where  she realizes that everything that she  has experienced that has made up her  life that she loves it has led her here  has led her to her death but even  knowing that now and this is then she  does not want any of that change not any  of it and I find it a really interesting  empower and emotionally powerful moment  because usually when you get characters  getting some degree of acceptance of  their fate it's usually comes with a  sense of peace and she has a little bit  of that at the end but we don't lose the  sadness and the fact that she's scared  she's scared about what's gonna happen  but she would still rather have it end  like this and have the story be what it  was that she'd experienced then lose  that and I find that touching and  powerful and sad number 8 from doomsday  a wall apart but worlds away this is I  think like many sad moments also I think  funny moments as well this comes down to  the performances and and I think David  Tennant does a good job of just because  the doctor has experienced loss before  and I don't mean to underplay what Rose  meant to him but he's experienced loss  of this scale before and so his  expression of just again having to deal  with this and but then her desperation  pounding on this wall with the tears  streaming down that that is a strong  moment because she just 