r Amy  losing the baby this one is very similar  to other moment with Donna from a couple  of spots ago and I I honestly couldn't  tell you why this one hits me a little  harder then that one does but it's very  much for the same reason ie I I have a  parent and there's there's something  about her crouching and huddling and  holding to protect this infant and you  know she is she was there being the  protective caring mother and in an  instant it's just gone everything she  thought she was defending and protecting  is just gone and her reaction and she  screams and she did a really good job of  capturing the shock the pain it felt  different from Donna's pain Donna's pain  and I'm not saying one pain is more  impactful than another but I think it  distinguishes it in terms of the moments  Amy's pain felt more like the pain of a  stillbirth or a miscarriage whereas  Donna's felt more like the loss of a  parent whose children they had for some  time and  they are they are they impact characters  differently and I I even knowing that  difference I I don't know if that's part  of the reason this one hits me harder  but I think it's just I think it's just  the moment at which it happens it's it  is such a rug pull whereas maybe the  Donna moment was more you see it coming  and you know what's gonna hurt me see  coming  whereas Amy it just out of nowhere maybe  that maybe that's the difference but  yeah that one hurts number two Wilfred  Mott trying to give the doctor a gun  this one is really hard to articulate  why I choke up every single time I see  this I well it's partially hard to  articulate it's hard to dissect how  about that because I think it comes down  to Bernard Cribbins performance as Wilf  and that he is choking his way through  these lines trying to give the doctor a  weapon and knowing that he probably  knowing the doctor won't take it but  just desperately wanting to help to do  anything he can to keep the doctor alive  and that he can't even get through the  sentence without starting to cry and  starting to blubber and that that  performance and how deeply will feels  the impending loss of David Tennant as  the doctor and I think personally that  that that moment is more powerful than  when the doctor actually saves him and  you know sacrifices himself so wolf can  live and he ends up dying ayat this  moment of him trying to give him the gun  is much much more lump in my throat  chokes me up than then later  again maybe it's the build-up versus  more of an in-the-moment thing because  it does feel like Wilfs breakdown and  tears weren't weren't built up to they  just started hitting him in that moment  and then they end up hitting me  and that's kind of that's kind of it and  finally the number one saddest moment  for me in New Era Doctor Who to date is  the Mindwipe of Donna from journey's end  and again it's down to performance it's  down to Catherine Tate and in in many  ways it recalls when River dies and she  she snaps at him you know don't you dare  change a thing  Donna Donna makes the same choice that  River does which is that she would  rather die having experienced what she  experienced then live and lose that the  difference is the doctor takes away her  ability to make that choice for herself  he makes that choice for her regardless  of what she wants and her begging her  shaking her head saying no no please as  he goes in to remove every memory of  what she had experienced with him and  her knowing it's coming in her begging  him not to do it that that is brutal and  that just kills me every single time it  and and most of these moments I've  talked about are things that now hit me  harder as I've gotten older as opposed  to when a lot of them first aired the  this is one that this this was hard when  the first era it's probably gotten  harder since then but this is one that  even even on first viewing it really got  to me and it stuck with me and it is in  my estimation the saddest moment  modern-e